1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,265
You are lying at the bottom of the deepest

2
00:00:02,265 --> 00:00:04,326
place on Earth. Almost eleven kilometres

3
00:00:04,326 --> 00:00:06,130
of water are sitting on top of you.

4
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:09,178
The pressure down here is about eleven hundred

5
00:00:09,178 --> 00:00:11,078
times what you feel on the surface.

6
00:00:11,078 --> 00:00:13,304
Roughly eight tonnes on every square inch

7
00:00:13,304 --> 00:00:14,010
of your body.

8
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:15,988
And here's the twist.

9
00:00:15,988 --> 00:00:18,843
You're not flat. You're not a little cube.

10
00:00:18,843 --> 00:00:21,698
From the outside, you look surprisingly...

11
00:00:21,698 --> 00:00:21,970
you.

12
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:25,244
So what actually happened on the way down?

13
00:00:25,244 --> 00:00:27,385
Which parts of you gave up first?

14
00:00:27,385 --> 00:00:30,045
And who's that, coming over to say hello?

15
00:00:30,045 --> 00:00:32,380
Let's find out, one depth at a time.

16
00:00:32,930 --> 00:00:35,295
You're on a ship in the western Pacific,

17
00:00:35,295 --> 00:00:37,838
a few hundred kilometres southwest of Guam.

18
00:00:37,838 --> 00:00:40,499
Under you is the Challenger Deep, the deepest

19
00:00:40,499 --> 00:00:42,510
known point of the Mariana Trench.

20
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:45,553
The average ocean is about three point seven

21
00:00:45,553 --> 00:00:47,762
kilometres deep. This spot is about ten

22
00:00:47,762 --> 00:00:50,028
point nine. You could drop Mount Everest

23
00:00:50,028 --> 00:00:52,294
in here and its peak would still be more

24
00:00:52,294 --> 00:00:54,050
than two kilometres underwater.

25
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:57,460
Now, people float. So for this experiment,

26
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:59,844
you're holding a very heavy anchor.

27
00:00:59,844 --> 00:01:02,500
Don't ask why. You took one big breath,

28
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:03,590
and you fell in.

29
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:06,584
The water up here is warm and tropical.

30
00:01:06,584 --> 00:01:09,467
And the second your face goes under, something

31
00:01:09,467 --> 00:01:10,720
ancient switches on.

32
00:01:11,270 --> 00:01:13,098
It's called the diving reflex.

33
00:01:13,098 --> 00:01:15,473
When cold water touches your face, your

34
00:01:15,473 --> 00:01:18,336
heart rate drops by ten to twenty-five percent,

35
00:01:18,336 --> 00:01:20,712
and blood vessels in your arms and legs

36
00:01:20,712 --> 00:01:23,271
squeeze shut to save oxygen for your brain

37
00:01:23,271 --> 00:01:23,880
and heart.

38
00:01:24,430 --> 00:01:26,757
Ten metres down, the pressure is already

39
00:01:26,757 --> 00:01:28,734
double what it was at the surface.

40
00:01:28,734 --> 00:01:31,061
And everything in you that's full of air

41
00:01:31,061 --> 00:01:32,050
starts to shrink.

42
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:34,258
Your ears feel it first.

43
00:01:34,258 --> 00:01:37,091
A sharp, stabbing pain, as water pressure

44
00:01:37,091 --> 00:01:38,749
pushes on your eardrums.

45
00:01:38,749 --> 00:01:41,513
Divers clear their ears every few metres

46
00:01:41,513 --> 00:01:44,760
for this reason. You are not clearing anything.

47
00:01:45,310 --> 00:01:47,635
Air-filled spaces get hit hardest.

48
00:01:47,635 --> 00:01:50,575
Your middle ears, your sinuses, your lungs,

49
00:01:50,575 --> 00:01:53,515
and your gut. Doctors call this barotrauma.

50
00:01:53,515 --> 00:01:55,430
You can call it the squeeze.

51
00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:57,012
About a minute in.

52
00:01:57,012 --> 00:01:59,478
Let's say you're sinking at about one metre

53
00:01:59,478 --> 00:02:01,771
per second. That's a bit faster than the

54
00:02:01,771 --> 00:02:04,351
first submarine that ever reached the bottom,

55
00:02:04,351 --> 00:02:05,670
back in nineteen sixty.

56
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:08,447
You're not running out of oxygen yet.

57
00:02:08,447 --> 00:02:10,795
What's really hurting is carbon dioxide

58
00:02:10,795 --> 00:02:12,361
building up in your blood.

59
00:02:12,361 --> 00:02:15,130
Every second, the urge to breathe gets louder.

60
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:18,218
Then you hit what's called the breath hold

61
00:02:18,218 --> 00:02:20,696
breakpoint. You can't hold it any longer.

62
00:02:20,696 --> 00:02:22,630
Your body takes a breath anyway.

63
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:25,907
Sometimes the throat slams shut first, a

64
00:02:25,907 --> 00:02:28,294
spasm that tries to keep water out.

65
00:02:28,294 --> 00:02:29,930
It doesn't usually last.

66
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:33,222
With no oxygen reaching your brain, you

67
00:02:33,222 --> 00:02:35,964
black out. There's no dramatic warning.

68
00:02:35,964 --> 00:02:37,300
It just goes quiet.

69
00:02:37,850 --> 00:02:40,828
Your heart stops. Around six minutes without

70
00:02:40,828 --> 00:02:43,873
oxygen, brain damage becomes something nobody

71
00:02:43,873 --> 00:02:46,716
recovers from. And from here on, it's just

72
00:02:46,716 --> 00:02:49,220
your body, going on a very long trip.

73
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:52,158
At two hundred metres, you pass through

74
00:02:52,158 --> 00:02:53,689
the last useful sunlight.

75
00:02:53,689 --> 00:02:56,261
Below this point, there isn't enough light

76
00:02:56,261 --> 00:02:58,220
for anything to photosynthesize.

77
00:02:58,770 --> 00:03:01,231
You've also just passed the deepest anyone

78
00:03:01,231 --> 00:03:03,633
has ever gone on a single breath and come

79
00:03:03,633 --> 00:03:06,328
back up under their own power, for the record.

80
00:03:06,328 --> 00:03:08,672
Herbert Nitsch, two hundred and fourteen

81
00:03:08,672 --> 00:03:10,430
metres, in two thousand seven.

82
00:03:10,980 --> 00:03:13,644
In two thousand twelve he reached two hundred

83
00:03:13,644 --> 00:03:15,598
and fifty-three metres on a sled.

84
00:03:15,598 --> 00:03:18,262
Minutes after surfacing, he developed serious

85
00:03:18,262 --> 00:03:20,808
decompression sickness. Even the best human

86
00:03:20,808 --> 00:03:22,880
ever at this is paying for it here.

87
00:03:23,430 --> 00:03:26,087
At seven hundred and one metres, you pass

88
00:03:26,087 --> 00:03:29,003
another human record. In nineteen ninety-two,

89
00:03:29,003 --> 00:03:31,789
a diver named Theo Mavrostomos reached that

90
00:03:31,789 --> 00:03:34,834
pressure in a test chamber in France, breathing

91
00:03:34,834 --> 00:03:36,260
a special gas mixture.

92
00:03:36,810 --> 00:03:38,431
He stayed for two hours.

93
00:03:38,431 --> 00:03:41,064
Below about a hundred and fifty metres,

94
00:03:41,064 --> 00:03:43,900
divers breathing helium mixes can get high

95
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:46,263
pressure nervous syndrome. Tremors.

96
00:03:46,263 --> 00:03:49,302
Jerking. Nausea. Their nerves start misfiring

97
00:03:49,302 --> 00:03:50,720
under pressure alone.

98
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:53,870
Not that you're doing much nerve firing

99
00:03:53,870 --> 00:03:56,537
right now. A local resident drifts past,

100
00:03:56,537 --> 00:03:59,270
takes one look at you, and decides you're

101
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:00,670
not its problem. Yet.

102
00:04:01,220 --> 00:04:03,635
One kilometre. Total darkness.

103
00:04:03,635 --> 00:04:06,532
No sunlight reaches this deep, ever.

104
00:04:06,532 --> 00:04:09,510
Scientists call it the midnight zone.

105
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:13,052
Up top, the tropical water was above twenty

106
00:04:13,052 --> 00:04:15,974
degrees. Down here it's about five or six.

107
00:04:15,974 --> 00:04:18,758
The warmth left in your body is draining

108
00:04:18,758 --> 00:04:20,010
away into the sea.

109
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:23,364
The pressure is now about a hundred atmospheres.

110
00:04:23,364 --> 00:04:25,642
Whatever air was left in your lungs has

111
00:04:25,642 --> 00:04:28,095
been squeezed to around a hundredth of its

112
00:04:28,095 --> 00:04:30,665
surface size. Your chest has caved in around

113
00:04:30,665 --> 00:04:30,840
it.

114
00:04:31,390 --> 00:04:34,142
But here's the answer to our big question.

115
00:04:34,142 --> 00:04:36,959
Most of you is water, around sixty percent.

116
00:04:36,959 --> 00:04:39,252
And water barely compresses at all.

117
00:04:39,252 --> 00:04:42,004
Even four kilometres down, it only shrinks

118
00:04:42,004 --> 00:04:43,380
by about two percent.

119
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:47,074
So the deep sea doesn't flatten you like

120
00:04:47,074 --> 00:04:49,982
a can. It goes after the air pockets.

121
00:04:49,982 --> 00:04:52,026
Lungs, sinuses, ears, gut.

122
00:04:52,026 --> 00:04:55,406
The rest of you just gets a firm, permanent

123
00:04:55,406 --> 00:04:55,720
hug.

124
00:04:56,270 --> 00:04:57,531
Around an hour in.

125
00:04:57,531 --> 00:05:00,264
Four kilometres. This is the abyss, the

126
00:05:00,264 --> 00:05:03,207
zone between four and six thousand metres.

127
00:05:03,207 --> 00:05:06,010
You're now deeper than the average ocean

128
00:05:06,010 --> 00:05:06,430
floor.

129
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:09,602
And at six kilometres, you enter the hadal

130
00:05:09,602 --> 00:05:12,473
zone, named after Hades, the Greek underworld.

131
00:05:12,473 --> 00:05:14,970
Only the deep trenches go this far down.

132
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:18,462
At about eight thousand metres, you meet

133
00:05:18,462 --> 00:05:21,624
the deepest fish ever filmed in the Mariana

134
00:05:21,624 --> 00:05:24,493
Trench. A snailfish. Pale, soft, almost

135
00:05:24,493 --> 00:05:27,508
see-through. It looks like a tadpole made

136
00:05:27,508 --> 00:05:28,170
of jelly.

137
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:31,195
Its trick is a molecule called TMAO, which

138
00:05:31,195 --> 00:05:34,024
helps hold its proteins in shape under pressure.

139
00:05:34,024 --> 00:05:36,322
The deeper a fish lives, the more of it

140
00:05:36,322 --> 00:05:36,970
they carry.

141
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:40,359
Researchers predicted fish hit a limit around

142
00:05:40,359 --> 00:05:42,504
eight thousand two hundred metres.

143
00:05:42,504 --> 00:05:45,406
Deeper than that, the chemistry stops working.

144
00:05:45,406 --> 00:05:48,055
No fish has ever been found in the deepest

145
00:05:48,055 --> 00:05:49,380
quarter of the ocean.

146
00:05:49,930 --> 00:05:52,383
The deepest fish ever filmed anywhere was

147
00:05:52,383 --> 00:05:54,956
seen in two thousand twenty-three, at eight

148
00:05:54,956 --> 00:05:57,649
thousand three hundred and thirty-six metres,

149
00:05:57,649 --> 00:05:59,025
in a trench near Japan.

150
00:05:59,025 --> 00:06:00,760
Right at the predicted limit.

151
00:06:01,310 --> 00:06:03,918
You, on the other hand, have no TMAO.

152
00:06:03,918 --> 00:06:06,668
You've got a breakfast you never got to

153
00:06:06,668 --> 00:06:08,430
digest. You keep sinking.

154
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:11,616
Nine kilometres. In nineteen sixty, two

155
00:06:11,616 --> 00:06:14,724
men passed this exact depth inside a submarine

156
00:06:14,724 --> 00:06:17,495
called Trieste. And right about here, one

157
00:06:17,495 --> 00:06:20,401
of its outer window panes cracked and shook

158
00:06:20,401 --> 00:06:21,550
the whole vessel.

159
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:24,864
They kept going. Jacques Piccard and Don

160
00:06:24,864 --> 00:06:27,628
Walsh. Their descent took four hours and

161
00:06:27,628 --> 00:06:29,010
forty-seven minutes.

162
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:32,456
About three hours after you fell in, you

163
00:06:32,456 --> 00:06:35,424
touch down. Ten thousand nine hundred and

164
00:06:35,424 --> 00:06:38,320
thirty-five metres. The water is between

165
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:39,840
one and four degrees.

166
00:06:40,390 --> 00:06:42,821
The floor is soft, pale ooze, made of the

167
00:06:42,821 --> 00:06:45,194
microscopic shells of tiny plankton that

168
00:06:45,194 --> 00:06:47,566
have been drifting down for thousands of

169
00:06:47,566 --> 00:06:49,760
years. You land in it like a cushion.

170
00:06:50,310 --> 00:06:53,329
Your new neighbours include xenophyophores,

171
00:06:53,329 --> 00:06:55,787
single cells bigger than your hand.

172
00:06:55,787 --> 00:06:57,612
One cell. Ten centimetres.

173
00:06:57,612 --> 00:06:59,930
And they don't even have a brain.

174
00:06:59,930 --> 00:07:03,019
So, honestly, you have a lot in common right

175
00:07:03,019 --> 00:07:03,300
now.

176
00:07:03,850 --> 00:07:05,537
And then the guests arrive.

177
00:07:05,537 --> 00:07:08,472
Little shrimp-like scavengers called amphipods.

178
00:07:08,472 --> 00:07:10,971
Scientists' baited traps down here bring

179
00:07:10,971 --> 00:07:12,470
up huge numbers of them.

180
00:07:13,020 --> 00:07:15,665
In two thousand fourteen, cameras in the

181
00:07:15,665 --> 00:07:18,840
Mariana Trench even filmed supergiant amphipods,

182
00:07:18,840 --> 00:07:20,956
much bigger than the usual ones.

183
00:07:20,956 --> 00:07:23,667
Down here, a body is a once in a lifetime

184
00:07:23,667 --> 00:07:24,130
buffet.

185
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:27,142
And here's the bleakest fact in this video.

186
00:07:27,142 --> 00:07:29,376
When researchers checked amphipods from

187
00:07:29,376 --> 00:07:31,781
the deepest trenches, every single one had

188
00:07:31,781 --> 00:07:34,071
at least one piece of synthetic material

189
00:07:34,071 --> 00:07:34,930
in its stomach.

190
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:38,012
You fell eleven kilometres to the most remote

191
00:07:38,012 --> 00:07:39,138
place on the planet.

192
00:07:39,138 --> 00:07:41,220
And our plastic still got here first.

193
00:07:41,770 --> 00:07:44,473
When big bodies, like whales, reach the

194
00:07:44,473 --> 00:07:47,454
deep sea floor, they become feeding grounds

195
00:07:47,454 --> 00:07:50,158
for whole communities of creatures, for

196
00:07:50,158 --> 00:07:52,237
years. You're a smaller snack.

197
00:07:52,237 --> 00:07:53,832
But it's the same idea.

198
00:07:53,832 --> 00:07:56,050
Nothing down here goes to waste.

199
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:59,328
In nineteen sixty, Piccard and Walsh spent

200
00:07:59,328 --> 00:08:01,211
twenty minutes on the bottom.

201
00:08:01,211 --> 00:08:02,510
Then they went home.

202
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:04,630
You are staying a bit longer.

203
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:07,928
So, the deep ocean doesn't crush you into

204
00:08:07,928 --> 00:08:10,743
a cube. It drowns you near the top, chills

205
00:08:10,743 --> 00:08:13,558
you on the way, squeezes your air pockets,

206
00:08:13,558 --> 00:08:16,239
and then serves you to the locals with a

207
00:08:16,239 --> 00:08:18,518
side of microplastics. Five stars.

208
00:08:18,518 --> 00:08:19,590
Would not visit.

209
00:08:20,140 --> 00:08:22,371
Next week, you get hit by three hundred

210
00:08:22,371 --> 00:08:24,544
million volts. Most people survive it.

211
00:08:24,544 --> 00:08:26,889
That's not the good news you think it is.

212
00:08:26,889 --> 00:08:27,690
See you there.
